            <h2>Can you buy a headstone before someone dies?</h2>
            <p>Yes — and it is more common in Southern Utah than most families realize. Pre-planning a headstone, sometimes called "pre-need" ordering in the monument industry, means designing, approving, and sometimes paying for a memorial while the intended recipient is still living.</p>
            <p>There are two main scenarios:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Companion monument planning.</strong> A couple purchases their cemetery plots together and wants to design the shared headstone while both are living. The stone is set after the first death — with the first spouse's dates engraved and a space reserved for the second — or it may be set with both names and birth years, leaving the death dates to be added later.</li>
              <li><strong>Individual pre-planning.</strong> A person of any age decides they want to control the memorial their family will use rather than leaving the decision to grieving survivors. Common among recently retired couples in Ivins, Santa Clara, and the Bloomington Hills area of St. George.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Pre-planning is completely legal, does not require any special permits beyond the standard cemetery approval process, and is welcomed by every municipal cemetery we work with in Washington County.</p>
