            <h2>Why Southern Utah families pre-plan more than most</h2>
            <p>Washington County's demographic is unusual in ways that push more families toward pre-planning:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>The snowbird pattern.</strong> Many couples split six months in St. George and six months back in Idaho, California, Nevada, or Oregon. When one spouse passes — wherever that happens — the surviving spouse is often in another state, dealing with grief while trying to coordinate a Southern Utah burial. Families who pre-planned the design and approved a proof before that moment avoid an enormous amount of logistical stress. Everything is already done. A death date needs to be added and the stone needs to be set.</li>
              <li><strong>The retirement community concentration.</strong> Ivins, Santa Clara, Sun River, Bloomington Hills — Washington County's retirement communities have one of the highest concentrations of couples in their 70s and 80s of any county in the Intermountain West. Pre-planning in this demographic is a normal, practical conversation, not a morbid one.</li>
              <li><strong>LDS temple sealing culture.</strong> Latter-day Saint couples who are sealed in the temple often have a theological comfort with death and an intentionality about memorial planning that drives pre-planning decisions. The headstone is part of the eternal family record, not just a marker. Couples choose to design it together.</li>
              <li><strong>The companion monument decision.</strong> A companion headstone — one stone for two lives — is much easier to design when both people can weigh in. The temple choice, the engraving style, the granite color, the epitaph: these are deeply personal decisions that surviving spouses often struggle to make alone under grief. Designing the stone together while both are living is one of the most practical gifts couples in Southern Utah give each other.</li>
            </ul>
