            <h2>How the pre-planning process works</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Cemetery verification first.</strong> We start every order — including pre-need orders — by calling the sexton at your cemetery to confirm the current rules for your specific plot. Cemetery rules change. A section that allowed uprights five years ago may have new restrictions. We verify before we quote.</li>
              <li><strong>Design consultation.</strong> We talk through granite color, headstone type (upright, flat, slant, bench, companion), engraving choices, and any symbols that matter to the family. For companion stones, this conversation includes where each name will appear, how dates will be handled, and whether the stone will be set before or after the first death.</li>
              <li><strong>Digital proof.</strong> We produce a digital layout showing the exact design at real scale — granite color, letter placement, any temple silhouettes or engravings. This is reviewed and approved by the family. Nothing goes to production until this sign-off is in writing.</li>
              <li><strong>Payment options.</strong> Pre-planned monuments can be paid in full or on a payment plan. We are flexible. If you are locking in current pricing for a stone you expect to need in two to five years, we can structure that conversation.</li>
              <li><strong>Setting timeline.</strong> A companion stone is typically set after the first death — at which point the first set of dates is final-engraved and the stone is set with the death date included, with the second side's death date blank. Some families set the stone with both names and birth years while both are living, then add death dates as they occur. We discuss both approaches and the cemetery's rules for each.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-stone-dark p-8 prose-custom">
            <h2>Companion monuments — the most common pre-need decision in Southern Utah</h2>
            <p>Companion headstones — one monument for two people — are the most common pre-planned memorial we design in Washington County. The typical scenario: a couple in their late 60s or 70s, both living, who have purchased adjacent plots at Tonaquint, Ivins, or Santa Clara and want to design the stone together.</p>
            <p>Key decisions for companion pre-planning:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Single stone or two stones.</strong> A companion monument is one wide stone spanning both plots. Some couples prefer two separate stones, side by side, with individual designs. The cemetery's rules for the specific plots determine which configurations are permitted — we verify before suggesting either.</li>
              <li><strong>Layout of names and dates.</strong> Most companion stones show the husband's name on the left and wife's name on the right, each with birth date and a blank space for the death date. Some couples prefer a single shared epitaph in the center with names flanking it. We provide design options and you choose.</li>
              <li><strong>What happens at the first death.</strong> When the first spouse passes, we return to the stone (or arrange for it to be returned to the fabricator if the addition is complex) and engrave the death date. The setting fee for this return visit is included in our pre-planned pricing.</li>
              <li><strong>Temple engravings for companion stones.</strong> We frequently design companion stones with both the St. George Utah Temple and the Red Cliffs Utah Temple on a single stone — one temple for each generation's endowment home. This is one of the most meaningful design choices available to Southern Utah Latter-day Saint families right now.</li>
            </ul>
